{"version_id": 85, "article_id": 9, "level_code": "en_c1", "lang": "en", "level": 5, "level_label": "C1 高级", "title": "Fairer rules needed to keep global science collaboration alive amid conflict", "title_zh": "冲突之下，全球科学合作要维持下去，就得有更公平的规则", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 218, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "Scientific collaboration has long been seen as a realm that rises above geopolitics. But as global tensions seep into international research, a troubling side effect is taking hold: institutional affiliation is increasingly read as a proxy for political allegiance.", "lead_zh": "长期以来，科学合作被视为能够超越地缘政治的一块净土。然而，随着全球紧张局势不断渗入国际科研，一种令人担忧的副作用正在显现：机构归属越来越被当作政治立场的代名词。", "preview": "Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. Yet, as global tensions incre", "preview_zh": "长期以来，科学合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益左右国际科研，一个意想不到的后果正在浮现：机构归属正在变成政治身份的代名词。这种转变的风险在于，研究人员受惩罚不是因为他们做了什么，而是因为他们恰好在哪儿工作。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 75, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e302484/content/audio/9/en_c1.mp3", "duration": 96.65, "size_bytes": 583776, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 245, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e302484/content/data/versions/85.json", "paragraphs": ["Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. Yet, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. This shift risks penalising researchers not for their work but for where they happen to be based.", "The authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University, argue in a Nature correspondence that the current drift demands fairer rules. Without them, the collaborative networks that have driven so much scientific progress could fracture along geopolitical fault lines, leaving entire regions isolated from the global research enterprise.", "The piece appears against a backdrop of mounting friction. Related coverage points to academic boycotts, hints of shifting research partnerships, and China's rethink of collaboration amid geopolitical tensions. Meanwhile, rising numbers of Asian and African PhD students are choosing to study in China, underscoring how mobility and geopolitics are becoming ever more entangled.", "The authors declare no competing interests. Their intervention is a timely reminder that science, however insulated it may aspire to be, cannot escape the political weather. The question, they suggest, is not whether geopolitics will intrude on research, but whether the rules governing collaboration can be made equitable enough to keep the enterprise open."], "paragraphs_zh": ["长期以来，科学合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益左右国际科研，一个意想不到的后果正在浮现：机构归属正在变成政治身份的代名词。这种转变的风险在于，研究人员受惩罚不是因为他们做了什么，而是因为他们恰好在哪儿工作。", "文章作者——上海大学的 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和浙江师范大学的 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi——在《自然》的一封通讯中指出，当前的这种趋势需要更公平的规则。若没有这样的规则，曾推动大量科学进展的合作网络就可能沿着地缘政治的断层线破裂，使整个地区被孤立于全球科研事业之外。", "这篇文章出炉之际，摩擦正不断加剧。相关报道指向学术抵制、研究伙伴关系可能生变的迹象，以及中国在地缘政治紧张背景下对合作的重新思考。与此同时，越来越多的亚洲和非洲博士生选择到中国求学，这凸显出人员流动与地缘政治正变得愈发纠缠不清。", "作者声明不存在利益冲突。他们的发声及时提醒人们：科学无论多么渴望置身事外，终究逃不开政治气候。他们暗示，问题不在于地缘政治是否会侵入科研，而在于规范合作的规则能否被制定得足够公平，从而让这项事业保持开放。"], "body": "Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. Yet, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. This shift risks penalising researchers not for their work but for where they happen to be based.\n\nThe authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University, argue in a Nature correspondence that the current drift demands fairer rules. Without them, the collaborative networks that have driven so much scientific progress could fracture along geopolitical fault lines, leaving entire regions isolated from the global research enterprise.\n\nThe piece appears against a backdrop of mounting friction. Related coverage points to academic boycotts, hints of shifting research partnerships, and China's rethink of collaboration amid geopolitical tensions. Meanwhile, rising numbers of Asian and African PhD students are choosing to study in China, underscoring how mobility and geopolitics are becoming ever more entangled.\n\nThe authors declare no competing interests. Their intervention is a timely reminder that science, however insulated it may aspire to be, cannot escape the political weather. The question, they suggest, is not whether geopolitics will intrude on research, but whether the rules governing collaboration can be made equitable enough to keep the enterprise open.", "body_zh": "长期以来，科学合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益左右国际科研，一个意想不到的后果正在浮现：机构归属正在变成政治身份的代名词。这种转变的风险在于，研究人员受惩罚不是因为他们做了什么，而是因为他们恰好在哪儿工作。\n\n文章作者——上海大学的 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和浙江师范大学的 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi——在《自然》的一封通讯中指出，当前的这种趋势需要更公平的规则。若没有这样的规则，曾推动大量科学进展的合作网络就可能沿着地缘政治的断层线破裂，使整个地区被孤立于全球科研事业之外。\n\n这篇文章出炉之际，摩擦正不断加剧。相关报道指向学术抵制、研究伙伴关系可能生变的迹象，以及中国在地缘政治紧张背景下对合作的重新思考。与此同时，越来越多的亚洲和非洲博士生选择到中国求学，这凸显出人员流动与地缘政治正变得愈发纠缠不清。\n\n作者声明不存在利益冲突。他们的发声及时提醒人们：科学无论多么渴望置身事外，终究逃不开政治气候。他们暗示，问题不在于地缘政治是否会侵入科研，而在于规范合作的规则能否被制定得足够公平，从而让这项事业保持开放。", "text": "Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. Yet, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. This shift risks penalising researchers not for their work but for where they happen to be based. The authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University, argue in a Nature correspondence that the current drift demands fairer rules. Without them, the collaborative networks that have driven so much scientific progress could fracture along geopolitical fault lines, leaving entire regions isolated from the global research enterprise. The piece appears against a backdrop of mounting friction. Related coverage points to academic boycotts, hints of shifting research partnerships, and China's rethink of collaboration amid geopolitical tensions. Meanwhile, rising numbers of Asian and African PhD students are choosing to study in China, underscoring how mobility and geopolitics are becoming ever more entangled. The authors declare no competing interests. Their intervention is a timely reminder that science, however insulated it may aspire to be, cannot escape the political weather. The question, they suggest, is not whether geopolitics will intrude on research, but whether the rules governing collaboration can be made equitable enough to keep the enterprise open.", "vocab": [{"word": "transcend", "pos": "verb", "zh": "超越，超脱", "note": "常与 geopolitics、boundaries 等搭配，强调不受限制。"}, {"word": "proxy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "代名词，替代指标", "note": "a proxy for 表示“某事物的代表或替代”。"}, {"word": "affiliation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "隶属关系，机构归属", "note": "institutional affiliation 指所属机构。"}, {"word": "unintended consequence", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "意想不到的后果", "note": "政策或趋势带来的非预期结果。"}, {"word": "fault lines", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "断层线，分歧点", "note": "比喻社会或政治中的裂痕。"}, {"word": "fracture", "pos": "verb", "zh": "破裂，分裂", "note": "可用于网络、联盟等抽象事物。"}, {"word": "entangled", "pos": "adj", "zh": "纠缠在一起的", "note": "be entangled with 表示与……紧密交织。"}, {"word": "friction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "摩擦，冲突", "note": "mounting friction 指不断加剧的摩擦。"}, {"word": "backdrop", "pos": "noun", "zh": "背景", "note": "against a backdrop of 是常见搭配。"}, {"word": "boycott", "pos": "noun", "zh": "抵制", "note": "academic boycott 指学术抵制。"}, {"word": "insulated", "pos": "adj", "zh": "隔绝的，不受影响的", "note": "常与 from 搭配。"}, {"word": "intrude", "pos": "verb", "zh": "侵入，干扰", "note": "intrude on/into 表示闯入、干涉。"}, {"word": "equitable", "pos": "adj", "zh": "公平的，公正的", "note": "比 fair 更正式，多用于制度、规则。"}, {"word": "timely reminder", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "及时的提醒", "note": "常用于评论性文章。"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "body_original": "CORRESPONDENCE\n06 October 2026\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules\nBy\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\n0\n&\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\n1\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\nShanghai University, Shanghai, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\nZhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nScientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity.\nAccess options\nAccess through your institution\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n$32.99\n/ 30 days\ncancel any time\nLearn more\nSubscribe to this journal\nReceive 5